          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The application of mathematics in the theory of music in the West is generally thought to start with Pythagoras and progress though the works of Euler, Mersenne, Helmholtz, and others. Recently,
            %strong mathematical music theory
            has gained impetus through, among others, the Neo-Riemannian theory of harmony, David Lewin’s research on transformational theory, and Guerino Mazzola’s application of topos theory.
          %p
            As well, the application of science and math to the theory of human perception of
            %span.newWikiWord
              sound
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/sound"} ?
            is known as
            %em psychoacoustics
            and has had a major impact in today’s musically digital world. All of these viewpoints have greatly enhanced the variety of mathematical concepts employed in the study of music that today include tools from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dihedral+group"} dihedral group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+analysis"} harmonic analysis
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Music+of+the+Spheres"} Music of the Spheres
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+formula+of+myth"} canonical formula of myth
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acoustics"} acoustics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pitch-class"} pitch-class
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                John Baez:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em This Week’s Finds (Week 234)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week234.html"} link
            %li
              %p
                n-Café:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Mathematics of Music at Chicago
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/05/the_mathematics_of_music_at_ch.html"} link
            %li
              %p
                For applications of mathematics to music, see
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~tmfiore/1/music.html"} Thomas M. Fiore's page
            %li
              %p
                For discussions on the interaction of category theory and music, see the
                %a{:href => "http://recherche.ircam.fr/equipes/repmus/mamux/"} MaMux seminar
                at IRCAM, Paris.
          %h2#references References
          %p A comprehensive anthology of Riemannian and Neo-Riemannian music theory is:
          %ul
            %li
              Edward Gollin, Alexander Rehding (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Oxford Handbook of Neo-Riemannian Music Theories
          %p An excellent textbook on the mathematics of music that ranges from Fourier analysis over digital music to compositional symmetries is
          %ul
            %li
              David J. Benson,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Music - A Mathematical Offering
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://homepages.abdn.ac.uk/d.j.benson/pages/html/music.pdf"} pdf
          %p Comparable in range though presumably more digestible for the non mathematical reader is
          %ul
            %li
              Gareth Loy,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Musimathics - the mathematical foundations of music. Vols. 1,2
          %p Some canonical music theory texts that rely on mathematics:
          %ul
            %li
              Allen Forte,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Structure of Atonal Music
          %blockquote
            %p “The Structure of Atonal Music ranks as one of the most important contributions to music theory in the twentieth century. His use of the computer as well as traditional means of analysis has led to fuller knowledge of musical structure and thereby enhanced both the understanding and the enjoyment of music.”
          %ul
            %li
              David Lewin,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Generalized Musical Intervals and Transformations
          %blockquote
            %p “David Lewin’s Generalized Musical Intervals and Transformations is recognized as the seminal work paving the way for current studies in mathematical and systematic approaches to music analysis.”
          %ul
            %li
              Joseph N. Straus,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to Post-Tonal Theory (third edition)
          %blockquote
            %p “The third edition stays abreast of recent theoretical developments by including discussions of transformational networks and graphs, contour theory, atonal voice leading, triadic post-tonality (including neotonality), inversional symmetry, and interval cycles.”
          %ul
            %li
              Arnold Schönberg,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theory of Harmony: 100TH ANNIVERSARY EDITION
          %p A nice first introduction to music theory and what groups can do for it is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alissa+Crans"}> Alissa S. Crans
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+M.+Fiore"} Thomas M. Fiore
              = succeed "," do
                %em Musical Actions of Dihedral Groups
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.1873"} abstract
          %p More mathematically inclined are the following
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Emilio Lluis-Puebla, Ocatavio A. Augustín-Aquino (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Memoirs of the Fourth International Seminar on Mathematical Music Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://sociedadmatematicamexicana.org.mx/SEPA/ECMS/resumen/PME1_1.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Guerino Mazzola,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrie der Töne
            %li#Mazzola02
              %p
                Guerino Mazzola,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Topos of Music
            %li
              %p
                Guerino Mazzola,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Music Theory: Status Quo 2010
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#LPAA10"} Lluis-Puebla, Augustín-Aquino (2010)
            %li
              %p
                Dimitri Tymoczko,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Geometry of Music: Harmony and Counterpoint in the Extended Common Practice
            %li
              %p
                Dmitri Tymoczko,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalizing Musical Intervals
                %strong 53
                no.2 (2009) pp.227-254. (
                %a{:href => "http://dmitri.mycpanel.princeton.edu/files/publications/lewin.pdf"}> draft
                )
          %p An introduction to group theory with applications to neo-Riemannian theory is
          %ul
            %li
              Ocatavio A. Augustín-Aquino, Janine du Plessis, Emilio Lluis-Puebla, Mariana Montiel,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Una introducción a la Teoría de Grupos con aplicaciones en la Teoría Matemática de la Música
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://pesmm.org.mx/Serie%20Textos_archivos/T10.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Besides the monumental
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Mazzola02"} Mazzola (2002)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Thomas Noll,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The topos of triads
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Colloquium on Mathematical Music Theory
                %strong 347
                (2005) 1-26 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228614514_The_Topos_of_Triads"}> researchgate
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Alexandre Popoff, Moreno Andreatta, Andree Ehresmann,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groupoids and wreath products of musical transformations: A categorical approach from poly-Klumpenhouwer networks
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematics and Computation in Music MCM 2019
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 11502
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://hal.science/hal-02392889v1"} hal-02392889v1
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-21392-3"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-21392-3
            %li
              %p
                Alexandre Popoff, Jason Yust:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Meter networks: a categorical framework for metrical analysis
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/17459737.2020.1836687"} doi:10.1080/17459737.2020.1836687
            %li
              %p
                Drew Flieder:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards a mathematical foundation for music theory and composition: a theory of structure
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/17459737.2024.2379788"} doi:10.1080/17459737.2024.2379788
          %p
            Methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            are employed to study tonality in
          %ul
            %li
              Peter beim Graben, Reinhard Blutner,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Toward a Gauge Theory of Musical Forces
              %strong 10106
              (2017). (
              %a{:href => "http://www.blutner.de/GrabenBlutnerQI16.pdf"}> draft
              )
          %p Hegel’s philosophy of music can be found in his lectures on aesthetics though these reflect to a large extent the view of his pupil Heinrich Gustav Hotho who compiled the posthumeous publication:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> G. W. F. Hegel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vorlesungen über die Ästhetik II
              = succeed "1985." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^4
          %p A development of the basic concepts of music theory along his ideas on dialectics can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moritz+Hauptmann"}> Moritz Hauptmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Die Natur der Harmonik und Metrik
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Moritz_Hauptmann"} wikisource
          %p
            The sonata form from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegelian
            perspective:
          %ul
            %li
              Ernst Nobbe,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Die thematische Entwicklung der Sonatenform im Sinne der Hegel’schen Philosophie betrachtet
          %p The following introductory texts on psychoacoustics help provide a foundation to better understand how sound becomes emotion becomes music:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Arthur H. Benade,
                %em Fundamentals of Musical Acoustics
                Dover New York 1976.
            %li
              %p
                Hugo Fastl, Eberhard Zwicker,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Psychoaccoustics: facts and models
            %li
              %p
                Juan G. Roederer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Physics and Psychophysics of Music
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^4
          %p A theory of emotions which applies beyond music in narrative arts is in
          %ul
            %li
              David Huron,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Sweet Anticipation - Music and the Psychology of Expectation
          %p
            An introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
            programming systematically employing examples from music is
          %ul
            %li
              Paul Hudak,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Haskell School of Music - From Signals to Symphonies
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.yale.edu/homes/hudak/Papers/HSoM.pdf"} pdf
          %p Other “mathemusical” topics include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Milton Babbit,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Function of Set Structure in the Twelve-Tone System
            %li
              %p
                E. Chew,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Towards a Mathematical Model of Tonality
            %li
              %p
                David Clampitt, Thomas Noll,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modes, the Height-Width Duality, and Handschin’s Tone Character
                %strong 17
                no.1 (2011).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+M.+Fiore"}> Thomas M. Fiore
                , Ramon Satyendra,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized Contextual Groups
                %strong 11
                no.3 (2005).
            %li
              %p
                J. Hook,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Uniform triadic transformations
                %strong 46
                no.1-2 (2002) pp.57–126.
            %li
              %p
                T. A. Johnson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations of Diatonic Theory: A Mathematically Based Approach to Music Fundamentals
            %li
              %p
                Fred Lerdahl,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Tonal Pitch Space
            %li
              %p
                Miguel F. M. Lima, J. A. Tenreiro Machado, António C. Costa,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Multidimensional Scaling Analysis of Musical Sounds Based on Pseudo Phase Plane
            %li
              %p
                Thomas Noll,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sturmian sequences and morphisms: a music theoretical application
            %li
              %p
                R. N. Shepard,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrical approximations to the structure of musical pitch
                %strong 89
                no.4 (1982) 305.
            %li
              %p
                J. Tennenbaum,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Foundations of Scientific Musical Tuning
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Manual On the rudiments of Tuning and Registration
            %li
              %p
                J. S. Walker, D. W. Gary,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematics and Music: Composition, Perception, and Performance
